the country is against  holding these olympics mike armstrong  global news  the u.s state department and centers for  disease control have issued a warning to  americans about the uk  the u.s has raised the warning to the  highest level level four telling  americans to  avoid travel to the united kingdom  because there is a very high level of  covid19 there  the cdc says if you must travel to the  uk make sure you are  fully vaccinated but it warns there is  still the potential  to get sick in india the number of  people who have died from covid19  may be nearly 10 times higher than the  official death toll  a new study by the u.s based center for  global development  estimates excess deaths that's the gap  between those recorded and those that  would have been expected  is between 3 and 4.7  million officially india is reporting  more than 414 000 people died from coven  it's believed overwhelmed hospitals may  have missed recording many deaths  in the u.s there was a fiery exchange  today between dr  anthony fauci and republican senator  rand paul  fauci the top infectious disease expert  in the u.s  denied he lied to congress about u.s  funding  what's called gain of function research  at the wuhan  virology lab in china research ran paul  claims  increased the transmissibility of  viruses and resulted in the deaths of  millions of people  this was fauci's reaction  senator paul you do not know what you  are talking about  quite frankly and i want to say that  officially  and you are implying that what we did  was responsible for the deaths of  individual  i totally resent that and if anybody is  lying here senator  it is you  i had one on one with prime minister  justin trudeau what he says about  accountability for indigenous children  harmed by the residential school system  it's likely there will be a federal  election soon and we now know one  prominent person who won't be running  mark carney the former governor of the  bank of england and the bank of canada  told the canadian press he is not going  to run there was much speculation carney  would turn to politics but he says he's  committed to helping organize the  private financial sector ahead of  november's  u.n climate conference he says he  doesn't rule out running at some point  in the future and says he supports prime  minister justin trudeau  far nasser sat down with trudeau to talk  about election planning  while the country faces a reckoning over  residential schools  you've been traveling quite a bit  recently a lot of people say you're  campaigning  this is what summer looks like for a  politician to get out there and meet  with people  especially after a pandemic where we've  been working really hard to support  people to have people's backs through  the pandemic and now people are  expecting us to be part of building back  better  so are you not meeting with advisors  talking about an election calling your  staff back from  the minority government there's always  uh there's always times where we have to  be ready for whatever comes but  we're focused on continuing to deliver  for canadians every step of the way  i want to focus right now on  islamophobia the national council of  canadian muslims says since the london  terror attack there has been a violent  incident against a muslim  every day why canada why do you think  that's happening here  we are seeing a rise of hatred a rise of  intolerance  fed in some places by populism and by  politics of division what about policy  though prime minister i mean you  represent the writing of papano it's  such a diverse writing  but there are people in your writing who  feel like you're not standing up to that  for them when it comes to  bill 21 do you honestly feel like you're  doing the best  in your power to stand up for those  people okay you know first of all let me  be very clear  okay i am opposed to that law and  everybody in papua new  most people across the country know uh  that i am in total disagreement with  bill 21  uh i don't think it's for any government  particularly in a country that values  rights and liberties to dictate to a  woman what she can and cannot wear i  think that's just  wrong however we have a justice system  that is set up  so that individuals can take their  governments to court  do you think it's racist i think it is  uh i think it's the wrong thing to be  doing  i do not think it goes in the right  direction i want to move on to  residential schools because this has  been a reckoning as you know  across canada but you know the truth and  reconciliation commission  spent uh five years getting testimony  from these survivors and now we have  these possible  crime scenes in multiple provinces yet  virtually no one has been held  accountable for what's happening you  said  and now we have these crime scenes no  those crime scenes have been there for  generations and we've known about them  so generations and indigenous peoples  have known about them for generations  what about accountability prime minister  what about the criminal charges there's  nobody's being charged would you like to  see that would you like to see criminal  accountability  what i want to see is secondary  to what the communities need yes  absolutely i will support anything that  families need to move forward because  you have to remember  that even as uh indigenous  kids were being told that they had no  worth they had no value in those  residential schools  and every other school in canada kids  were being told non-indigenous kids were  being told  that indigenous communities had no worth  had no value  so reconciliation isn't just about  indigenous people and the government  right  it's about all of us and we all have a  role to play  okay prime minister thank you so much  for your time pleasure  you can watch pharah's full interview  with the prime minister on our website  globalnews.ca globalnational  next the woman who waited decades to  defy gravity  for the second time this month a  multi-billionaire has made it to the  edge of space in his  rocket amazon founder jeff bezos flew  107 kilometers above the earth's surface  and lived to tell the tale  and he brought three people with them  including 82 year old wally funk  an accomplished pilot she's one of 13  women who thanks to private funding  volunteered to undergo astronaut  training in the 1960s  she excelled at it but the training was  cancelled and the women were excluded  from nasa's space program  funk has waited six decades for this  moment  i've been waiting a long time to finally  get it up there  and i've done a lot of astronaut  training through the world  and i could always beat the guys on what  they were doing because i was always  stronger and i've always done everything  on my own it was wonderful i want to go  again  fast jackson prosco looks at  billionaires in their rockets and  whether the money could be better spent  here on earth  this might be the most important  delivery jeff bezos has ever completed  a gentle drop-off in the texas desert  after a ride to the edge of space  my expectations were high and they were  dramatically exceeded  52 years to the day after humans first  walked on the moon  blue origin's new shepard rocket roared  into the sky  making history by carrying both the  oldest and youngest passengers into the  record books  18 year old oliver damon of the  netherlands and 82 year old wally funk  finally getting her glimpse of space up  close  i i loved every minute of it i just wish  it a little longer  all told the crew experienced about four  minutes of weightlessness  while the world's richest man became the  latest billionaire to stake a claim to  space  joining the ranks of richard branson and  elon musk  i want to thank every  amazon employee and every amazon  customer  because you guys paid for all this bezos  sees space  tourism as a path to space exploration  it's also a lucrative business catering  to the ultra wealthy  the company claims to have sold 100  million dollars worth of tickets for  future launches  at an undisclosed price per seat the  price is going to be set by the market  demand  so right now the only data point we have  is that somebody was willing to pay 28  million dollars  for the first blue origin ticket for  that price a lucky few can now see the  world the way only a few hundred people  ever have  a prospective past astronauts have  described as life-changing  when you go into space and see how  fragile it is you'll want to take care  of it even more  like his fellow space billionaires bezos  has  big dreams to one day explore the moon  and beyond  back on planet earth these baby steps  are for now  all about the business of space tourism  one small step  for those who can afford it jackson  prosko global news  washington  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is the sunset at remick rapids  park  in ottawa thanks for watching hope to  see you here again tomorrow take care of  yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]  you
 

